## 2. Core System Identity

The marketing site is a **multi-sided acquisition engine**. Most companies build a website and call it marketing. USRad has built something architecturally distinct: a system that operates different conversion machines simultaneously for audiences with fundamentally different motivations, decision timelines, and information needs — all from the same codebase.

This distinction matters because it changes how the system should be evaluated, invested in, and maintained. A website is a communication tool. An acquisition engine is a growth system. The difference is whether the architecture is designed around content or around conversion outcomes.

What the marketing system actually does:

**Demand generation** for the patient audience. When an uninsured patient searches for "MRI cost near me" and lands on USRad, the system's job is to reduce the cognitive distance between "this looks interesting" and "I'm searching for my procedure." Every element of the homepage — the headline, the search interface, the pricing callouts, the trust signals — is calibrated to move the patient to that one action. The SmartMatch branding, the animated loading overlay, the procedure-grouped search results — these are not decoration. They are conversion architecture.

**Supply formation** for the provider audience. The provider funnel is not advertising the platform to imaging centers — it is actively constructing a geographic network one facility at a time. The Network Map page exists to show a specific imaging center operator that their territory is either already occupied or still available. That is not a marketing message. That is a real-time market signal designed to trigger a specific decision: apply before the window closes.

**Decision engineering** for both audiences. SmartMatch™ as a concept — applied to both patients and providers — is a decision simplification system. It tells patients "we've already evaluated your options and here is the best one." It tells providers "here is exactly how you will be ranked and what drives that ranking." In both cases, it converts an ambiguous choice into a guided commitment. That is the work of a decision engine, not a marketing page.

