## 8. Conversion System Analysis

### Where Conversion Is Strong

The patient funnel's search-to-Remix handoff is architecturally tight. The five-parameter query construction is deterministic, the loading overlay bridges the application boundary gracefully, and the SmartMatch branding creates favorable expectations before the patient sees results. The critical conversion moment — procedure selection — is handled by a purpose-built search engine with relevance scoring, not a generic dropdown. This is a meaningful investment in the most important moment in the patient funnel.

The provider funnel's three-page persuasion sequence — network map, SmartMatch explainer, portal tour — is genuinely differentiated acquisition architecture. Most marketplace platforms send a cold email with a pitch deck. USRad has built three distinct tools, each addressing a specific provider objection, that together walk a prospect from awareness to decision without requiring any human sales intervention. This is scalable acquisition infrastructure.

The employer funnel's lead scoring system transforms passive downloads into a prioritized pipeline. Most B2B marketing systems count form submissions and call them leads. USRad's system scores behavior across multiple actions, enabling sales to focus on leads that have demonstrated sustained intent rather than one-time curiosity.

### Where Conversion Leaks

The most significant conversion leak is the dual provider funnel. The `/provider` pages use one design language (CarbonLayout, Inter font, white background, navy and gold palette). The `/imaging-center` pages use a different design language (PartnerPageLayout, Manrope font, cream background). Both target the same audience: imaging center operators considering joining the USRad network. A provider who encounters both will experience the brand as inconsistent. More damaging, the two funnels likely split attribution, dilute SEO authority for "imaging center partnership" queries, and create internal confusion about which funnel to invest in.

The non-functional chat widget is a credibility problem at scale. A "Need help?" button on every page that does nothing is a trust failure. For a patient who is anxious about medical cost and is making a significant financial decision, discovering that a support channel doesn't work creates doubt about the platform's operational quality. This is not a minor UX issue — it is an active conversion impediment.

The employer schedule page uses the deprecated layout with a different font and background than the employer landing page it follows. A prospect who completes the ROI calculator on the employer page and clicks through to schedule a consultation encounters a visually inconsistent interface. This is not catastrophic, but in a B2B context where credibility signals matter, it undermines the polished impression the rest of the funnel creates.

### Intentional vs Accidental Friction

The search-to-Remix navigation is intentional friction — it requires a full page load, but the loading overlay transforms that latency into a perceived value moment. This is good friction that should not be eliminated.

The gated employer implementation guide is intentional friction — requiring a work email filters out non-qualified leads. This is good friction that should not be eliminated.

The magic link email verification in the booking flow is intentional friction — it establishes email ownership before committing a patient to a booking. This is good friction with room for UX improvement.

The dual provider funnel is accidental friction — it was not designed, it accumulated. The non-functional chat widget is accidental friction — it was scaffolded and never connected. The employer schedule layout inconsistency is accidental friction — it is technical debt creating a business problem.

