## 12. Cross-App Dependency Risk

The USRad platform is architecturally split between two applications: the Astro marketing site and the Remix booking/portal application. This split is appropriate — it allows each application to be optimized for its specific role. But the coupling between them creates a specific category of risk that requires active management.

### The Query Parameter Contract

The patient conversion funnel depends on five URL parameters passing correctly from the Astro search form to the Remix search results page. These parameters encode the patient's procedure selection, their ZIP code, and display metadata. If any parameter is renamed, removed, or reformatted in either application, the handoff breaks — the patient arrives at a Remix page that cannot build a valid search query, and the search returns no results.

This is not a theoretical risk. It is a brittle contract between two codebases that are developed and deployed independently, by developers who may not be coordinating changes at the parameter level. There is no automated test that validates this contract, no schema that documents it as a shared interface, and no deployment gate that would catch a parameter mismatch before it reaches production.

### The Header/Footer Sync Problem

The navigation headers and footers on the Astro site and the Remix application are separate implementations that are supposed to be kept in sync manually. They last synced in December 2025. This is not just a visual consistency issue — it is a governance problem. There is no defined process for keeping them aligned, no automated check that flags drift, and no ownership assigned to maintaining the sync. The gap will continue to widen unless a deliberate decision is made to either unify the components or establish a formal sync process.

### The REMIX_URL Variable

Twenty or more files in the Astro codebase reference the Remix application's base URL through a single environment variable. If this variable is misconfigured in any deployment environment — staging, preview, production — every cross-app link and API call fails simultaneously. This is appropriate centralization in theory, but it creates a single point of failure that is not guarded by any validation layer. A deployment with a missing or incorrect `PUBLIC_REMIX_URL` would silently break patient search, provider signup, and employer email delivery at once.

### The Network Map Iframe

The most visually impactful element of the provider acquisition funnel — the live network map — is served by the Remix application as an embedded iframe. If the Remix application has a deployment issue, a route change, or a performance degradation, the most persuasive element of the most important provider page shows a loading spinner indefinitely. There is no fallback, no static screenshot, and no error state that would preserve the page's conversion function during a Remix outage.

