## 13. Strategic Recommendations

### 1 · Install sitemap.xml and robots.txt This Week

This is the highest-leverage, lowest-effort SEO improvement available. Installing `@astrojs/sitemap` takes hours. Not having it has been costing organic search discovery for 15+ months. Every new page — the network map, the SmartMatch explainer, the portal tour — is being discovered by search engines through link traversal rather than direct declaration. The robots file is equally critical: without it, debug pages, test pages, and provider portal pages are indexable and consuming crawl budget. This should have been done before the v2 audit. It must be done now.

### 2 · Consolidate the Dual Provider Funnel

The `/imaging-center` pages should be deprecated and redirected to their `/provider` equivalents. Two funnels targeting the same audience with different design systems is a dilution of SEO authority, brand coherence, and development investment. The unified `/provider` funnel with its coordinated persuasion sequence — landing page, network map, SmartMatch, portal tour — is architecturally superior. The `/imaging-center` pages should serve as 301 redirects, not competing destinations.

### 3 · Replace the Non-Functional Chat Widget

Either connect the chat widget to a real support channel — even a simple email-to-support fallback — or remove it entirely. A placeholder that does nothing is worse than nothing. On a platform where patients are making healthcare decisions and providers are evaluating a business commitment, a broken support signal actively undermines the credibility the funnel is designed to build.

### 4 · Fix the Static Fake Data on the Provider Teaser

Update the `MarketOpportunityTeaser` component to display live network statistics from the database, or remove the specific numbers entirely and replace them with qualitative statements that don't require accuracy. Showing "5 USRad partners" to a provider who then sees hundreds of pins on the live network map is a credibility gap that happens at the top of the funnel.

### 5 · Formalize the Query Parameter Contract Between Astro and Remix

Document the five search parameters as a versioned API contract. Create an automated test — even a simple end-to-end check — that validates the patient handoff works correctly after any deployment to either application. The patient conversion funnel depends entirely on this contract. Its current informal status is disproportionate to its commercial importance.

### 6 · Establish a Header/Footer Sync Process

Either extract the shared navigation components into a shared package that both applications consume, or establish a quarterly sync review process with assigned ownership. The current approach — sync when someone remembers — guarantees continued drift. At minimum, create a documented checklist of the elements that must match and assign someone to verify it after any navigation change in either codebase.

### 7 · Remove Debug Endpoints from Production

The three debug API endpoints should be gated behind authentication or removed from the production deployment immediately. This is a security and competitive intelligence risk that requires no architectural decision — only a deletion or an auth gate. It should be resolved in the next deployment.

### 8 · Move Lottie to On-Demand Loading

250KB of animation library loading on every CarbonLayout page — including pages with no animations — is a performance tax with a straightforward solution: load Lottie only on pages that use it. This change improves Core Web Vitals scores on every marketing page and reduces the payload cost for every patient and provider who visits the site. Better page speed scores also directly influence search ranking.

### 9 · Add Dynamic Open Graph Tags to CarbonLayout

Social sharing from any USRad page currently shows the same title and description — the generic homepage metadata hardcoded in CarbonLayout. When a patient shares a specific procedure page, or a provider shares the SmartMatch explainer on LinkedIn, the social preview shows the wrong content. Astro's per-page metadata system makes this straightforward to fix. Dynamic OG tags are particularly high-value for the provider acquisition pages, which are likely to be shared by imaging center operators evaluating the network.

### 10 · Build a Network Map Fallback for Remix Outages

The network map iframe should have a graceful degradation state — a static screenshot of the map with a "Live map temporarily unavailable" message — that displays when the Remix application cannot be reached. Currently, a Remix outage during peak provider acquisition hours shows the most important element of the most important provider page as an empty loading state. A static fallback preserves the page's conversion function and the provider's experience.

